From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Abe Massry <amassry@linode.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:34:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] system: run QEMU as a WASI reactor

Link QEMU as a WASI reactor that the host drives one main loop
iteration at a time, rather than as a command whose main() never
returns.  Between calls no wasm frames are live, so the host can move
the instance to a fresh wasmtime Store by copying its memory, which is
the only way to free compiled TB modules.  Exports (system/main.c):

  wasi_qemu_init()       parse the command line, start the machine
  wasi_qemu_step()       one main loop iteration; returns -1 to go
                         on, else the exit status
  wasi_qemu_jit_reset()  forget all compiled TBs (after a Store move)
  wasi_qemu_quit()       quit cleanly: power off, flush and close the
                         disk images

- runstate.c: qemu_main_loop_step() runs one iteration: the main loop
  (sleeping only while the vCPUs are idle), frames from the user
  network, a vCPU time slice and pending RCU callbacks.
  QEMU_STATUS_EVERY=<secs> prints the vCPU state periodically, for
  debugging long runs.
- vl.c: an unreadable default config file is not an error (there is
  no /etc/qemu in the sandbox); register the wasi-gfx display, which
  is compiled from qemu-wasi's display/ directory; "user" is the
  default network, as when slirp is available.
- monitor.c: no QMP dispatcher coroutine, which cannot work with
  coroutines that never suspend.  The HMP monitor works.
